### Page 3 of 6

```text
[CA]
Preliminary Plat for Alliance Addition, 4th Filing, a replat of a portion of Lot 1, Block 1,
Alliance Addition, Cheyenne, Wyoming (located at the northwest corner of the intersection of
Ridge Road and Jackson Street) – referred to Public Services Committee.

2019-2020 Collective Labor Agreement between the City of Cheyenne and Cheyenne
Firefighters, Local Union No. 279, International Association of Firefighters.  Mr. White moved to
approve, seconded by Mr. Esquibel.  Mike O’Donnell, City Attorney, requested the item to be
postponed for 2 weeks as “Kelly Day” computation numbers need to be updated to reflect correct
numbers.  Dr. Rinne moved to postpone to 11/25/2019, seconded by Mr. White.  Upon inquiry,
Mr. O’Donnell explained the current Collective Labor Agreement for fiscal year 2019-2020 is
being finalized.  Motion to postpone carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body.

Land Purchase and Sale Agreement between the City of Cheyenne and Victory Real Estate
Management, LLC, for the purchase of real property described as a portion of Lot 1, Block 1,
P.A.S. Subdivision, City of Cheyenne, Laramie County, Wyoming, for the appraised value of
$329,630.00, for economic development purposes (General Fund).  Brendan Ames, Chief
Economic Development Officer, explained the agreement is for the Hitching Post site which was
previously approved, and this agreement for an amended amount.  He further explained the
purchase will allow the city to have access ownership to the property, will provide more parking
for the Ice and Events Center and will allow for future success of the site.  It was noted that the
purchase agreement for the Atlas Hotel will soon be finalized which will benefit economic
development. Mr. Ames further explained there are some parcels east of the site that are currently
under contract.  Upon inquiry, Bruce Trembath, Chief Building Official, reported he has been
researching prices for abatement and how to better secure the area.  Mr. Trembath advised
estimates include: $9,600.00 to board up structures, $14,000.00 for chain link fencing and
approximately $37,000.00 for 50% solar motion lighting.  He provided project phase abatement
estimates and noted estimates are for all buildings.  Upon inquiry, Mr. Trembath advised he will
provide figures provided by the seller on asbestos abatement and demolition.  Alessandra McCoy
Fakelman, Assistant City Attorney, advised the city is proposing to purchase 2 buildings (buildings
3,4 and 5 which consists of 2 physical structures) and noted that because contamination levels are
unknown, it is hard to provide abatement costs and some rehabilitation may be possible.  It was
noted that grant funding can be considered as possible funding source, however the city must own
the property to be eligible to apply.  Ms. McCoy Fakelman advised the city would be eligible for
a Brownsfield site grant after site stabilization and with the way the agreement is written and
because of a clean-up lien that is on the front 4 acres of the property, if those acres are to be
subdivided and sold, the city could collect the lien dollars to be used for abatement purposes.  Upon
inquiry, Ms. McCoy Fakelman reported there is not a purchase and sale agreement currently for a
neighborhood facility building and property.  Upon inquiry related to the neighborhood facility
building and property, Teresa Moore, Community Recreation and Events Director, reported
Laramie County School District # 1 is doing their due diligence and the first Environmental Site
Assessment report has been completed and is clear.  She noted they are looking at drainage studies
to make sure they can build on the site, then will look at negotiating a purchase and sale agreement
which is anticipated for the first of the year.  Mr. White moved to approve in an amount not to
exceed $329,632.00, seconded by Mr. Roybal.  Discussion included history of proposed purchase
and sale agreement, liability of purchase with many unknowns including funding sources,
```

